1
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:05,360
Hello, everyone, and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:06,220
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,760 --> 00:00:07,280
I'm Amira.

4
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:08,260
Hey, Amira.

5
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:09,080
How are you today?

6
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:10,040
I'm doing great.

7
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:10,720
What about you?

8
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,360
I'm doing pretty good.

9
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:16,020
I'm excited about this lesson that you have for us today.

10
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:19,160
Yes, it's actually a very, very useful lesson.

11
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:22,880
It's about someone asking his boss for an assistant.

12
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:23,840
Oh, okay.

13
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:24,340
An assistant.

14
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:25,240
What's an assistant?

15
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,540
Well, it's someone who helps you with your work around the office.

16
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Okay, great.

17
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:37,840
So let's listen to this dialogue, and when we come back, we'll take a look at all the interesting and useful vocabulary that you'll find.

18
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:52,080
Like I told you before, we just don't have the resources to hire you an assistant.

19
00:00:52,820 --> 00:00:55,900
I understand that, but the fact is, we're understaffed.

20
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:57,740
The timing is just not right.

21
00:00:58,220 --> 00:01:00,920
The economy's bad, and it's too risky to take on new staff.

22
00:01:01,820 --> 00:01:02,640
Yeah, I guess you're right.

23
00:01:03,320 --> 00:01:03,920
Here's an idea.

24
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:05,380
What if we hire an intern?

25
00:01:06,180 --> 00:01:07,760
She would take some of the weight off my shoulders.

26
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:08,920
She?

27
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:10,180
Yeah, you know.

28
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:14,780
A recent graduate, she could give me a hand with some of these projects, and we could keep our costs down.

29
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,380
That sounds reasonable.

30
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:18,180
Let me see what I can do.

31
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:27,840
Tony, I'd like to introduce you to your new assistant.

32
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:29,420
Okay, great.

33
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:30,400
Let's meet her.

34
00:01:33,230 --> 00:01:33,850
Hi, I'm Adam.

35
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:36,750
Oh, hi, I'm Tony.

36
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:44,580
And we're back. Tony, he's surprised, huh?

37
00:01:44,880 --> 00:01:49,900
Yeah, he's, uh, I don't know why, though, he's surprised. I think we should talk about that a little bit later.

38
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:54,840
Yes, for now, let's look at some interesting vocabulary and phrases here.

39
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:57,040
Marco, I know you've chosen one for us.

40
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:00,320
Yeah, the first one that I want to take a look at is resources.

41
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:02,200
Resources.

42
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:03,480
Resources.

43
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:04,720
Resources.

44
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:08,400
And that means, in this text, basically money.

45
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:09,240
Right.

46
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:11,400
So the office doesn't have enough money.

47
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:17,560
Okay, I've chosen another interesting word for you, and that's understaffed.

48
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:18,900
Understaffed.

49
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:20,400
Understaffed.

50
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:21,880
Understaffed.

51
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:28,280
Now, understaffed means that they don't have enough people working there.

52
00:02:28,820 --> 00:02:30,080
They don't have enough workers.

53
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:32,360
Right, they don't have enough employees.

54
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:33,860
Perfect. Okay.

55
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:38,060
The next one is timing is just not right.

56
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:40,220
The timing is just not right.

57
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:43,100
The timing is just not right.

58
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,020
The timing is just not right.

59
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:55,240
Okay, let's listen to how we can use this entire phrase in a different situation, so then we can understand what it means.

60
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:56,140
Let's listen.

61
00:03:03,210 --> 00:03:05,770
I was going to sell my house, but the timing is just not right.

62
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:11,870
Honey, I think we should have a baby.

63
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:13,890
The timing is just not right.

64
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:20,490
Okay, great.

65
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:22,540
I guess that makes it clear.

66
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:27,640
Yes, actually, it's very obvious that it means that it's just not a good time.

67
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:28,220
Right.

68
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:28,800
Perfect.

69
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:34,180
I have another interesting phrase for you, which is weight off my shoulders.

70
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:36,200
Weight off my shoulders.

71
00:03:36,740 --> 00:03:38,460
Weight off my shoulders.

72
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:40,220
Weight off my shoulders.

73
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:50,180
So, to take weight off my shoulders is another way of saying to help me with my work or to take some of my work away from me.

74
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:57,440
If you have a lot of things to do and I come and help you, that means that I took some of the weight off your shoulders.

75
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:57,900
Yes.

76
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:02,780
Let's look at the next phrase that we have here, and it's give me a hand.

77
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:04,100
Give me a hand.

78
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:05,900
Give me a hand.

79
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:07,240
Give me a hand.

80
00:04:07,650 --> 00:04:09,430
Okay, now this is not literal.

81
00:04:09,690 --> 00:04:12,410
It's not that I'm going to give you somebody's hand, right?

82
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:18,730
Let's listen to some examples, and then we can come back and explain what it means.

83
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:27,400
Can you give me a hand with these boxes?

84
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:37,860
Neil, I'll need you to give me a hand with the sales reports.

85
00:04:44,460 --> 00:04:45,820
Here, let me give you a hand with your suitcase.

86
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:57,460
Alright, give me a hand is another way of saying help or to help out.

87
00:04:58,010 --> 00:04:59,530
Exactly, to help out.

88
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:00,060
Right.

89
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:00,460
Perfect.

90
00:05:00,750 --> 00:05:07,090
Now, the last phrase we're going to look at here in this dialogue is keep our costs down.

91
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:09,070
Keep our costs down.

92
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,420
Keep our costs down.

93
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:13,920
Keep our costs down.

94
00:05:14,430 --> 00:05:20,610
So, to keep our costs down means to try and not spend so much money.

95
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:21,530
Exactly.

96
00:05:21,860 --> 00:05:27,080
Many companies, for example, now control how much printing you do.

97
00:05:27,470 --> 00:05:27,750
Yes.

98
00:05:27,900 --> 00:05:28,140
Right.

99
00:05:28,290 --> 00:05:29,790
To keep the costs down of paper.

100
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:30,760
Yes.

101
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:35,900
So, in other words, they do not want to have unnecessary spending.

102
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:36,890
Perfect.

103
00:05:37,310 --> 00:05:51,310
Okay, I think it's time for us to listen to this dialogue one more time, but I want everyone to listen and see why Tony is surprised at the end when he meets his intern, when he meets his assistant.

104
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:06,060
Like I told you before, we just don't have the resources to hire you an assistant.

105
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:09,860
I understand that, but the fact is, we're understaffed.

106
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:11,700
The timing is just not right.

107
00:06:12,180 --> 00:06:14,900
The economy's bad and it's too risky to take on new staff.

108
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:16,620
Yeah, I guess you're right.

109
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:17,900
Here's an idea.

110
00:06:18,420 --> 00:06:19,320
What if we hire an intern?

111
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:21,800
She would take some of the weight off my shoulders.

112
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:22,900
She?

113
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:28,740
Yeah, you know, a recent graduate, she could give me a hand with some of these projects and we could keep our costs down.

114
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:30,420
That sounds reasonable.

115
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:32,160
Let me see what I can do.

116
00:06:39,590 --> 00:06:41,810
Tony, I'd like to introduce you to your new assistant.

117
00:06:42,690 --> 00:06:43,410
Okay, great.

118
00:06:43,910 --> 00:06:44,390
Let's meet her.

119
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:47,820
Hi, I'm Adam.

120
00:06:48,540 --> 00:06:50,900
Oh, hi, I'm Tony.

121
00:06:55,470 --> 00:07:03,630
Well, you know, Marco, I think that Tony was really surprised because he was kind of hoping to have a female intern.

122
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:06,650
Yeah, I think that's exactly what he was expecting.

123
00:07:07,090 --> 00:07:08,870
Maybe this guy is a little bit sexist.

124
00:07:09,330 --> 00:07:13,130
He thought that an intern should be a girl, but instead he was surprised.

125
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,140
Instead he got Adam.

126
00:07:14,610 --> 00:07:20,370
Exactly, and it looks like Adam is really big and really tall because he's got that deep voice.

127
00:07:20,930 --> 00:07:21,090
Right.

128
00:07:21,870 --> 00:07:25,010
An intern, Marco, do you want to elaborate on that?

129
00:07:25,450 --> 00:07:36,870
Working at a company with a very low salary or sometimes no salary at all, but what you're gaining is experience and you're learning a lot of things.

130
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:46,820
Because everyone who has been an intern or had an intern knows that sometimes they are not treated all that well.

131
00:07:46,990 --> 00:07:51,110
Sometimes they just serve coffee or they have to do paperwork.

132
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:53,520
Or like photocopying things.

133
00:07:53,860 --> 00:07:54,180
Exactly.

134
00:07:55,380 --> 00:08:01,140
But some internships are really, really cool and some are really good experiences for learning.

135
00:08:01,860 --> 00:08:02,680
Yes, definitely.

136
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:14,660
Actually, I know a friend of mine, she used to work as an intern in one of those really big companies and she told me that she has gained so much experience doing that.

137
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:30,220
Yeah, I mean, even if you don't get paid, the experience that you get is really good and that just opens doors for maybe getting another job or sometimes the interns get hired by the company to stay because they do such a good job.

138
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:33,180
Yeah, I think that's mostly the case, huh?

139
00:08:33,500 --> 00:08:34,820
Yeah, that happened to me once.

140
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:39,540
I started working as an intern and after three months I was hired by the company.

141
00:08:39,720 --> 00:08:41,240
So, it does happen.

142
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:42,640
So, you did a good job.

143
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:44,360
Uh, yeah, of course.

144
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:47,700
Alright, folks, we're out of time today.

145
